1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,959
Something crossed the Mediterranean

2
00:00:01,959 --> 00:00:03,028
and struck a detector

3
00:00:03,028 --> 00:00:03,741
three and a half

4
00:00:03,741 --> 00:00:05,595
kilometres down. One particle.

5
00:00:05,595 --> 00:00:06,350
It lit more than

6
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:07,105
a third of the

7
00:00:07,105 --> 00:00:08,238
sensors in a cubic

8
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:09,802
kilometre of water. We

9
00:00:09,802 --> 00:00:11,135
call them ghost particles,

10
00:00:11,135 --> 00:00:12,246
because they pass through

11
00:00:12,246 --> 00:00:14,175
planets without slowing. On

12
00:00:14,175 --> 00:00:16,888
the thirteenth of February,

13
00:00:16,888 --> 00:00:18,439
2023, KM3NeT caught one

14
00:00:18,439 --> 00:00:19,833
muon. Its angle and

15
00:00:19,833 --> 00:00:21,430
brightness imply a neutrino

16
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:22,428
of two hundred and

17
00:00:22,428 --> 00:00:24,601
twenty peta electronvolts. About

18
00:00:24,601 --> 00:00:25,887
thirty times the previous

19
00:00:25,887 --> 00:00:27,287
record. And the detector

20
00:00:27,287 --> 00:00:28,442
was barely ten percent

21
00:00:28,442 --> 00:00:29,810
finished. Here is the

22
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:31,600
problem. IceCube, at the

23
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:32,738
South Pole, has seen

24
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:34,085
nothing like it. Two

25
00:00:34,085 --> 00:00:35,461
detectors, one sky, a

26
00:00:35,461 --> 00:00:37,622
three sigma disagreement. Either

27
00:00:37,622 --> 00:00:39,012
we got extremely lucky,

28
00:00:39,012 --> 00:00:40,055
or the Standard Model

29
00:00:40,055 --> 00:00:41,937
is missing something. Sterile

30
00:00:41,937 --> 00:00:44,249
neutrinos. New interactions. Something

31
00:00:44,249 --> 00:00:45,720
that lets particles travel

32
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:46,771
further than they should.

33
00:00:46,979 --> 00:00:48,120
One flash, and a

34
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:49,261
crack in physics.
